{
  "id": "COMPOUND:192 - DISEASE:1932",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between succinylcholine and Macrostomia, mediated through the coiled-coil domain containing 33 (CCD33) protein. Succinylcholine, a neuromuscular blocker, is linked to CCD33 through a series of causal relationships, with CCD33 acting as a common intermediary in multiple conditions. These conditions include Melanoma-Pancreatic cancer syndrome, Hypophosphatemic rickets, and Glaucoma 1, open angle, O, all of which are associated with CCD33 dysfunction. The shared involvement of CCD33 in these diverse pathologies indicates a broader biological role, possibly in cellular signaling or structural maintenance, which may be disrupted by succinylcholine. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between succinylcholine and Macrostomia, though the exact nature of the interaction remains unclear. The consistency of CCD33 as a central node across these pathways strengthens the hypothesis, but further research is needed to elucidate the precise mechanism by which succinylcholine influences Macrostomia.",
